let __v_351 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"lumpy-starch\",\"Lumps in porridge, polenta, couscous or mash are dry grain or dry potato that never took up liquid: tip the grain into the liquid in a thin stream while you whisk, rather than adding the liquid to the grain, and stir the bottom of the pan as it comes up to the boil. A mash goes lumpy when it is mashed cold or with thin liquid: warm it through with a splash of hot milk and put it through a sieve, which takes out every lump in one go.\"],[\"lumpy-liquid\",\"A lumpy sauce is flour that cooked before it met the liquid, or liquid that went in faster than the whisk could follow: slake the flour or cornflour in cold water first, add the hot liquid in a thin stream, and keep the whisk flat on the bottom of the pan where the flour settles. Off the heat, a hard whisk and a sieve rescue a sauce that has already lumped - or strain it, put it back on the heat and start the thickening again with fresh roux, which is quicker than fighting the lumps.\"],[\"lumpy-blend\",\"Lumps in a blend are too little liquid under the blade and too short a run: add the liquid first, blend 2 to 3 minutes and scrape down twice, and thin it with iced water rather than oil. Anything with skins in it - chickpeas, tomatoes, nuts - will stay rough until it goes through a sieve.\"],[\"\",\"Lumpy food is dry powder or cold fat that met hot liquid too fast: mix the starch with cold water first, add it in a thin stream while you whisk, and keep the whisk flat on the bottom of the pan. Off the heat, whisk it hard and push it through a sieve. Tell me which dish it is and I can say what went wrong in that one.\"]]}")?;
frame.store("_LUMPY_FIX", __v_351.clone())?;
